# Low Poly Colorizer — Stride import notes

Generated by Low Poly Colorizer (LPC).
<https://github.com/wasdcat/low-poly-colorizer> — a WASDCAT Games project.

> **Experimental.** The Stride export is new and has seen little testing
> across Stride versions and projects. Please report problems.

This folder holds a ready-made **Stride 4** material and **two lookup
textures** for the LPC look:

| File | What it is |
|:---|:---|
| `{{prefix}}palette.png` + `{{prefix}}palette.sdtex` | one sRGB color per palette cell |
| `{{prefix}}preset_lut.png` + `{{prefix}}preset_lut.sdtex` | preset data: roughness (clearcoat folded in, see below) / metallic / clearcoat / emission in R / G / B / A |
| `{{prefix}}multicolor.sdmat` | the material for meshes painted in LPC |
| `{{prefix}}palette_cheatsheet.png` | the palette with its group, row and column names — for people, to look up cells |

The material is built from Stride's stock nodes (*Texture* color / scalar),
no custom shader: it opens, previews and compiles in Game Studio like any
material you made yourself.

## Setting up

1. **Export into your Stride project's `Assets` folder** (or a subfolder of
   it). Game Studio picks up the `.sdtex` / `.sdmat` assets on its own.
2. **Color space: Linear** (Game Settings ▸ Rendering ▸ Color Space — the
   default for new projects). The palette texture is sampled as sRGB, so in
   Linear space the colors match the LPC picker.
3. **The mesh**: export the painted model from Blender as FBX or glTF.
   `lpc_uv0` and `lpc_uv1` must be the first two UV maps (use *Fix UV Maps* in
   the add-on if they are not), so they arrive as `TEXCOORD0` / `TEXCOORD1`.
4. **Assign `{{prefix}}multicolor`** to the model's material slot. It reads the
   palette cell from `TEXCOORD0` and the preset from `TEXCOORD1` per face.

## Do not change the texture settings

Both `.sdtex` assets are written by every export. The material reads single
texels by cell, so Stride's defaults would corrupt them:

- **Compressed = off** — block compression would shift every palette color.
- **Generate mipmaps = off**, and the material samples with **Point**
  filtering — a data texture must never blend across cells.
- **Premultiply alpha = off** — `{{prefix}}preset_lut.png` stores emission in
  alpha; premultiplying would wipe roughness / metallic of every preset that
  does not glow.
- **sRGB sampling**: on for the palette, **off** for the preset LUT (raw data).

The same holds for the material: edit a copy if you want to experiment — the
next export overwrites `{{prefix}}multicolor.sdmat`.

## Stable asset IDs

Every exported asset carries a fixed GUID (`Id:`) that depends only on its
file name, so every export writes the same IDs: models and scenes referencing
`{{prefix}}multicolor` keep their material across re-exports, fresh checkouts
and other machines.

## What the material does

| Channel | Source |
|:---|:---|
| Diffuse | palette texture at `TEXCOORD0` (V flipped: LPC counts palette rows bottom-up) |
| Glossiness (inverted = roughness) | preset LUT, R — roughness with the clearcoat folded in |
| Metalness | preset LUT, G |
| Emissive | palette color × preset LUT A × {{emission_factor}} (the add-on's *Emission Factor*) |

The preset LUT is addressed with `u = position × {{preset_texel_scale}} + {{preset_texel_offset}}`
(the texel centers of {{preset_count}} preset(s)) — the texture nodes' *Scale* /
*Offset*. This is baked in at export time: re-export after adding or deleting
presets.

## Clearcoat is approximated

Stride's stock material has no clearcoat amount that could be driven per
face, so the export folds each preset's *clearcoat* into its roughness:
the LUT's R channel holds

    roughness + clearcoat × ({{clearcoat_roughness}} − roughness)

with {{clearcoat_roughness}} being the add-on's *Clearcoat Roughness*. A fully
coated preset is as smooth as the coat; half a coat lands halfway. It is one
highlight instead of two: a matte base under a glossy coat reads smoother in
Stride than in Blender or Godot, where the coat sits on top of the base. The
raw clearcoat stays in the LUT's B channel, unused by the material.
Re-export after changing *Clearcoat Roughness* or a preset's clearcoat.

**Not supported in Stride (yet):** there is no *singlecolor* material for
meshes not painted in LPC.
